[Music]  here's a story every parent should pay  attention to an alarming new study finds  that lead exposure is still a huge  problem among children in the u.s  between 2018 and 2020 quest diagnostics  tested more than one million children  for lead in their blood all were under  the age of six incredibly about half had  detectable amounts of lead in their  blood that's nearly six hundred thousand  children and get this kids in the  poorest areas of the country were nearly  two and a half times more likely to have  lead in their blood dr john lopez  joining us right now good morning so  what does lead exposure what does that  mean what type of impact does that have  on children well lead is a toxin  especially to the neurological system so  think about you have these developing  brains especially in young kids and it  can affect learning it can affect  behavior it can cause an increase in  adhd and then all sorts of other  problems hearing problems growth  problems um it really is a bad thing  it's a toxin and there's no cure  there's no cure for it once you have  these effects that they're they're  really not reversible you can give  people remedial learning and things like  that and at very high levels you can try  to do chelation therapy to lower the  levels in your blood but no once it  happens it's happened something nature  said stuck out to me that kids in the  poorest areas of the country were nearly  two and a half times more likely why is  it why is it more  why do you see more of it in poorer  areas as opposed to more affluent  sections you know one reason is is the  legacy of lead in these areas you know  the  lead in paint was not outlawed and not  banned until like 1978. you have these  old houses with chipped paint and kids  especially when they're like one or two  they tend to put a lot of things in  their mouth it's in the soil and it's  also in lead pipes as we found out in  flint michigan all right so as parents  are watching right now they're thinking  to themselves okay what are things that  i should look out for what are the main  sources of lead well they're right now  they're in probably the greatest sources  in the lead uh in the chipped paint  that's around you know we have this  legacy of of lead poisoning and it's all  over the place and even though it was  banned in 1978 it's still in these old  houses the lead pipes it's in the soil  it's in all sorts of other places and we  really need to you know get get our act  together and try to remove it but we can  do something about this right  you know we look at the statistics and  it doesn't sound good you we we all know  how you can get it now but can't we fix  this we can fix it so you know first of  all i spoke to dr harvey kaufman of  quest diagnostics he was a co-author and  they actually did the testing and they  found that uh there was lead present in  a little over 50 percent of the samples  which is astounding it's astounding now  it's not a representative samples but  still it's just too many people and you  know the good news is that the number of  the amount of lead in our systems have  come down dramatically like more than 90  percent since lead and gasoline was  removed and in pain over the past 40  years but the fact that it's still  present even in small amounts is not  very good because there is no safe level  so what dr mona hannah attisha told me  last night and remember she's the star  pediatrician from flynn michigan yeah  from flint michigan she exposed the  whole thing and she said you know she's  practically giddy because they're down  to the last few dozens of lead pipes  that need to be removed they know how to  do something about it they're teaching  other places they have remedial things  in the in place and they have a registry  that's following these kids but she said  why do we have to use these kids as  canaries in the coal mine  we know that it's out there there's this  legacy of lead we know where it is and  we should go out and clean it up  beforehand do prevention rather than  reacting to the kids later after they've  already had it and there's really  nothing the kids should be routinely  tested for lead even today in 2021 well  right now it used to be routinely tested  and the cdc stopped doing that about  1997 because they said oh the numbers  are coming down and they didn't i think  realize that even tiny amounts uh are  present as shown in this study so i do  actually think that it should be  routinely uh measured at one and two but  as uh as dr hannah tesha said why wait  till then we shouldn't use them as oh  look at that the levels are up let's do  something about it we know it's out  there we know it's a problem let's do  something about it right now and there  is legislation uh right now pending uh  with the infrastructure bill to remove  the lead pipes and that's nobody knows  how many there are maybe about 10  million there'll be there'll be billions  of dollars to do that but what about dr  phil landrigan who's who revealed all  this in the 70s he's a hero said to me  last night also he said  why not remove the paint chips too it  costs billions of dollars but it's going  to save many more than that john thank  you so much valuable information now if  you want to see  lead exposure a tip sheet from quest  diagnostics go to our website  cbsmornings.com it's also available in  spanish as well as english

